ction rule moves interior edge points toward feature-transform locations on the skeleton while keeping edge endpoints fixed at their node positions.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Attraction preserves node endpoints\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"x_new = [1 - alpha phi(lambda(x)/lambda(e_ei))] x + alpha phi(lambda(x)/lambda(e_ei)) FT_S(x), with phi(t)=[2 min(t,1-t)]''^K, so endpoint motion is zero and middle-edge motion is largest.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Restricting attraction to skeleton paths passing through endpoint feature points prevents sharp kinks caused by feature-transform singularities.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Path regularization prevents kinks\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"For each edge, the algorithm selects a skeleton path whose endpoints include the feature points of the edge endpoints; special points whose FT_S(x) lies off that path are mapped by arc-length interpolation onto the path fragment before attraction.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Repeated reclustering with decreasing similarity thresholds creates bottom-up bundling in which fine local bundles merge into coarser structures.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Iterative reclustering coarsens bundles\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"The paper starts with a high similarity threshold, decreases delta and reclusters every three to five iterations, producing fewer and larger clusters once earlier iterations have already made edges locally elongated and partially bundled.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"CUDA image-space distance transforms, skeletonization, path computation, and attraction make the skeleton-based edge bundling pipeline substantially faster than a CPU AFMM implementation on the tested graphs.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"CUDA accelerates skeleton bundling\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"The CUDA implementation takes about 4 milliseconds for distance, feature transform, and skeletonization on the same image, about 0.8 seconds per iteration for the graph in Figure 7a-b, and is reported as roughly 100 times faster than the CPU-based AFMM.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Smoothing, relaxation, and cushion shading let users adjust whether bundles appear as sharp organic branches or smoother force-directed-like curves while preserving the computed connectivity pattern.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Smoothing controls bundle structure\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"Smoothing removes the skeleton effect from bundles, relaxation makes bundles thicker with less effect on curvature, and cushion shading modulates saturation and brightness using distance to the skeleton to help users follow overlapping bundles.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Skeleton-based edge bundling is most appropriate for coarse-scale connectivity exploration because bundling overdraws individual edges and distorts edge directions.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Bundling trades detail for structure\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"The limitations section states that any bundling inherently destroys information because edges are overdrawn, cannot be identified separately, and edge directions are distorted, so it should be used for coarse-scale connectivity patterns.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_items\": [\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-skeleton-bundling-pipeline--a18f4c9d72e6\",\n      \"title\": \"Skeleton Bundling Pipeline\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"The method proceeds by clustering edges into geometrically similar groups, computing a thin distance-field shape around each cluster, extracting the shape skeleton and feature transform, attracting the cluster edges toward the skeleton, iterating the process, and finally smoothing and rendering the result with cushion-like shading.\",\n      \"overview\": \"This formal pipeline is the core evidence that SBEB treats skeletons as layout cues rather than merely as visual shading aids. It establishes how clustering, distance transforms, skeletonization, attraction, iteration, and rendering fit together, while leaving final visual quality dependent on graph layout, parameters, and subjective readability.\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"formal_argument\",\n      \"claim_refs\": [\n        \"0\",\n        \"4\",\n        \"6\"\n      ],\n      \"source_id\": \"source-03-skeleton-based-edge-bundling-for-graph-visualization--1a3dfa0e6b4c\",\n      \"page_refs\": [\n        \"Fig. 1\",\n        \"Sec. 2\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-edge-cluster-distance--bb37a9e42d10\",\n      \"title\": \"Sampled Edge Distance\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"The clustering section defines d(e_i,e_j)=sqrt(sum_{k=1..N} ||e_ik-e_jk||''^2) over uniformly spaced edge samples, with N in [50,100], and reports that hierarchical bottom-up agglomerative clustering with full linkage and this Euclidean distance gives the best results.\",\n      \"overview\": \"This equation-level evidence explains how the method obtains elongated, coherent edge groups before skeletonization. Because the metric compares full sampled trajectories rather than only endpoints, it supports bundling curved or spatially similar edges, but it also means cluster quality depends on the initial layout and sampling resolution.\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"equation\",\n      \"claim_refs\": [\n        \"1\",\n        \"4\"\n      ],\n      \"source_id\": \"source-03-skeleton-based-edge-bundling-for-graph-visualization--1a3dfa0e6b4c\",\n      \"page_refs\": [\n        \"Eq. 1\",\n        \"Sec. 2.1\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-shape-skeleton-definitions--c4f08d2b61a9\",\n      \"title\": \"Distance-Field Skeleton Definition\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"The shape around a cluster is defined as Omega={x in R''^2 | DT_Delta(C)(x) <= omega}; its skeleton S_Omega is the set of points in Omega admitting at least two distinct boundary feature points at distance DT_boundary(Omega)(x), and the skeleton feature transform FT_S maps a point to a nearest skeleton feature point.\",\n      \"overview\": \"These definitions are the mathematical basis for using a skeleton as the centerline of a cluster'\"'s inflated edge shape. They show why the bundling target is locally centered, although the construction also depends on the chosen inflation radius omega and on image-space discretization.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"equation\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"0\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-skeleton-based-edge-bundling-for-graph-visualization--1a3dfa0e6b4c\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"Eq. 2\\\",\n        \\\"Eq. 3\\\",\n        \\\"Eq. 4\\\",\n        \\\"Eq. 5\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-attraction-endpoint-preservation--d9256b38aaf4\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Endpoint-Preserving Attraction Rule\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"The attraction rule is x_new = [1 - alpha phi(lambda(x)/lambda(e_ei))]x + alpha phi(lambda(x)/lambda(e_ei))FT_S(x), where phi(t)=[2 min(t,1-t)]\"'^K. The text states that edge endpoints do not move, nearby points move less, and points near the middle move most.\",\n      \"overview\": \"This evidence directly supports the claim that SBEB bends edges into bundles without moving graph nodes. The rule provides controllable tightness through alpha and curvature behavior through K, but repeated application can still distort edge direction as a cost of bundling.\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"equation\",\n      \"claim_refs\": [\n        \"2\",\n        \"7\"\n      ],\n      \"source_id\": \"source-03-skeleton-based-edge-bundling-for-graph-visualization--1a3dfa0e6b4c\",\n      \"page_refs\": [\n        \"Eq. 7\",\n        \"Eq. 8\",\n        \"Sec. 2.4\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-feature-transform-regularization--f71a0c6e2b55\",\n      \"title\": \"Path-Based Singularity Regularization\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"For each edge, the algorithm selects a skeleton path passing through the feature points of both edge endpoints. If a point'\"'s FT_S(x) is not on that path, the point is treated as special and mapped to a corresponding point on the path by arc-length interpolation before the attraction formula is applied.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This procedural evidence explains how the method avoids discontinuities that would otherwise create sharp kinks when edges cross Voronoi-like feature-transform boundaries. It supports the robustness claim for smooth bundles, while depending on correctly detected skeleton tips and paths.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"3\\\",\n        \\\"6\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-skeleton-based-edge-bundling-for-graph-visualization--1a3dfa0e6b4c\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"Sec. 2.4.1\\\",\n        \\\"Fig. 3\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-iterative-parameter-schedule--0e613cab8f27\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Progressive Reclustering Schedule\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"The iterative algorithm begins with a high similarity threshold to obtain thin coherent clusters, then decreases delta and reclusters every three to five iterations so that already bundled edges form fewer, larger, locally elongated clusters. The authors also state that alpha decreases from 0.9 to 0.2 and that I=15 is used in practice.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This evidence establishes why SBEB is progressive: it first creates local bundles and then lets those bundles merge into coarser structures. The parameter schedule gives practical defaults, but it also shows that results are controlled by heuristic choices rather than by a single optimality theorem.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"model_constraint\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"4\\\",\n        \\\"6\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-skeleton-based-edge-bundling-for-graph-visualization--1a3dfa0e6b4c\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"Sec. 2.5\\\",\n        \\\"Sec. 3\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-cuda-performance-tables--e89d4a6c31b2\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"CUDA Performance Measurements\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"Table 1 reports total GPU times of 6.3 seconds for US airlines, 4.1 seconds for US migrations, 7.4 seconds for Radial, 29.2 seconds for France air, and 5.2 seconds for Poker. Table 2 reports CUDA timings of 2 ms for inflation, 8 ms for holes, 2 ms for skeletonization, less than 12 ms for paths, and 3 ms for attraction.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The table values ground the performance claim in reported measurements rather than only complexity arguments. They show seconds-scale complete runs on several graph datasets and millisecond-scale GPU primitives, though the results are tied to the authors' hardware, image size, and implementation choices.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"table_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"5\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-skeleton-based-edge-bundling-for-graph-visualization--1a3dfa0e6b4c\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"Table 1\\\",\n        \\\"Table 2\\\",\n        \\\"Sec. 3.1\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-limitations-information-loss--9ac3b7d85021\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Bundling Information Loss\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"The discussion states that any bundling inherently destroys information: edges are overdrawn, individual edges cannot be identified separately, and edge directions are distorted. The recommended use is for applications interested in coarse-scale connectivity patterns where explicit graph simplification cannot be applied.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This limitation evidence is important because it bounds the interpretation of the method's visual results. SBEB improves structural readability for large graphs, but the paper explicitly cautions that bundled drawings should not be treated as preserving all individual-edge information.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"7\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-skeleton-based-edge-bundling-for-graph-visualization--1a3dfa0e6b4c\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"Sec. 5\\\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"relations\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"1\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"0\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Coherent geometric clustering is required before cluster skeletons can serve as meaningful bundling targets.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"derived_from\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"2\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"0\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The attraction rule operationalizes the central idea of moving clustered edges toward skeleton centerlines.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"3\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"6\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Regularized path attraction helps make the resulting bundles smooth enough for postprocessed visual exploration.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"4\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"0\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Iterative coarsening makes skeleton-guided bundling work at multiple structural scales.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"7\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"6\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Because bundling loses individual-edge detail, controls for smoothing and relaxation are important for practical interpretation.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Skeleton-based edge bundling constructs bundled layouts for general graphs by clustering geometrically similar edges, inflating each cluster into a distance-field shape, extracting that shape's skeleton, and attracting edges toward skeleton paths. The method is implemented with image-space GPU operations and is presented as a fast, controllable way to reveal coarse connectivity structure while accepting the information loss inherent in edge bundling.\\\",\n  \\\"warnings\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"code\\\": \\\"missing_publication_date\\\",\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"Publication date could not be resolved from the source record; source_date_inference.date is null.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"source_dossier\\\": {\n    \\\"summary_short\\\": \\\"This source introduces skeleton-based edge bundling, which uses medial axes of inflated edge clusters as graph-layout guides. It combines sampled-edge clustering, distance-field skeletons, endpoint-preserving attraction, GPU acceleration, and visual postprocessing to reveal coarse connectivity while obscuring individual edge detail.\\\",\n    \\\"summary_long\\\": \\\"Ersoy, Hurter, Paulovich, Cantareira, and Telea propose skeleton-based edge bundling for visualizing large general graphs whose node positions are already known. The central idea is to turn groups of geometrically similar edges into thin image-space shapes and then use each shape's medial-axis skeleton as the centerline toward which the edges should be attracted. This reframes bundling as a sequence of edge clustering, distance-transform construction, skeleton extraction, skeleton-path selection, and endpoint-preserving edge advection. The paper's reasoning path is mathematical and procedural: sampled-edge distances create coherent clusters; distance fields inflate cluster drawings into shapes; skeletons identify local centers of those shapes; feature transforms provide attraction directions; and a regularization step prevents singular attraction artifacts from becoming visible kinks. The implementation emphasizes graphics hardware, using CUDA distance and feature transforms, hole filling, skeletonization, path computation, and independent edge attraction to achieve seconds-scale performance on several graph examples. The result is a method that can produce organic, branching bundles or smoother force-directed-like curves depending on smoothing and relaxation choices. Its practical interpretation is deliberately limited: the visual output helps users see coarse routes, topic-to-topic citation structure, air traffic corridors, or migration-like patterns, but it does not preserve individual edge identity or exact direction. The paper is therefore strongest as a layout and interaction technique for structural overview, not as a lossless representation of graph topology.\\\",\n    \\\"sections\\\": [\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Core Argument\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The paper argues that the skeleton of an inflated edge-cluster shape is a natural bundling guide because skeletons are locally centered within elongated shapes. Once similar edges are clustered, their surrounding distance-field shape captures a shared corridor, and its skeleton provides the central path along which those edges can be drawn.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"0\\\",\n          \\\"1\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Clustering And Shape Construction\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The first technical step compares uniformly sampled edge trajectories and uses hierarchical agglomerative clustering to find spatially close, same-direction edge groups. Each cluster drawing is then inflated by a distance threshold into a compact shape, making skeletonization possible even though the original input consists of graph edges rather than filled regions.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"1\\\",\n          \\\"0\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Attraction Mechanics\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The attraction formula moves sampled edge points toward skeleton feature-transform locations, with an arc-length weighting that leaves endpoints fixed and moves middle points most strongly. This is essential for graph drawings because nodes must remain at their original positions while edges bend into shared bundles.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"2\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Robustness Measures\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"A direct feature-transform advection field can create sharp kinks near discontinuities. The paper addresses this by assigning each edge to a skeleton path through its endpoint feature points and remapping special off-path attraction cases onto that path, making the attraction smoother and more stable.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"3\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Progressive Bundling\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"SBEB is not a one-shot operation: it reclusters repeatedly as edges move closer together. A high initial similarity threshold creates reliable fine-scale bundles, then decreasing thresholds allow those already coherent bundles to merge into larger structures without immediately grouping unrelated crossing edges.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"4\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Implementation And Performance\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The implementation treats shapes, skeletons, distance transforms, and attraction largely as image-space operations. Reported table measurements show millisecond-scale CUDA primitives and total GPU times of a few to tens of seconds across tested datasets, supporting the claim that hardware acceleration is central to the method's practicality.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"5\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Visual Control\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The paper gives users postprocessing controls over smoothing, relaxation, and cushion shading. These controls matter because skeleton-guided layouts can look sharply organic or smoother and more force-directed-like, and shaded profiles help users follow overlapping bundles without changing the computed screen-space thickness.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"6\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Limits And Interpretation\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The authors explicitly caution that bundling destroys information by overdraw, loss of individual-edge identity, and direction distortion. The method should therefore be interpreted as a tool for overview and coarse connectivity analysis, especially when graph simplification is unavailable or unsuitable.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"7\\\"\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\nJSON\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"mkdir -p /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/graphics/runs/run-20260426T164930Z--dj3neh2btz/semantic && cat > /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/graphics/runs/run-20260426T164930Z--dj3neh2btz/semantic/ingest_extraction.json <<'JSON'\n{\n  \\\"source_date_inference\\\": {\n    \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\n    \\\"date\\\": null,\n    \\\"origin\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\n    \\\"rationale\\\": null\n  },\n  \\\"source\\\": {\n    \\\"display_title\\\": \\\"Skeleton-Guided Edge Bundling for Graph Visualization\\\",\n    \\\"authors\\\": [\n      \\\"Ozan Ersoy\\\",\n      \\\"Christophe Hurter\\\",\n      \\\"Fernando V Paulovich\\\",\n      \\\"Gabriel Cantareira\\\",\n      \\\"Alexandru C Telea\\\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \\\"claims\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Skeletons of inflated edge-cluster shapes provide locally centered targets for bundling geometrically similar graph edges.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Skeletons guide edge bundles\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"The method clusters edges, constructs a thin distance-field shape around each cluster, computes the shape skeleton and skeleton feature transform, then attracts the clustered edges toward that skeleton.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Hierarchical agglomerative clustering with full linkage and Euclidean distances between uniformly sampled edge points yields clusters of geometrically close edges that follow graph structure.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Sampling clusters similar edges\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"The edge distance is defined over uniformly spaced samples along the edges, d(e_i,e_j)=sqrt(sum_k ||e_ik-e_jk||\"'^2), with N in [50,100], and the authors report that full-linkage HBA gives the best results.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"The attraction rule moves interior edge points toward feature-transform locations on the skeleton while keeping edge endpoints fixed at their node positions.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Attraction preserves node endpoints\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"x_new = [1 - alpha phi(lambda(x)/lambda(e_ei))] x + alpha phi(lambda(x)/lambda(e_ei)) FT_S(x), with phi(t)=[2 min(t,1-t)]''^K, so endpoint motion is zero and middle-edge motion is largest.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Restricting attraction to skeleton paths passing through endpoint feature points prevents sharp kinks caused by feature-transform singularities.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Path regularization prevents kinks\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"For each edge, the algorithm selects a skeleton path whose endpoints include the feature points of the edge endpoints; special points whose FT_S(x) lies off that path are mapped by arc-length interpolation onto the path fragment before attraction.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Repeated reclustering with decreasing similarity thresholds creates bottom-up bundling in which fine local bundles merge into coarser structures.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Iterative reclustering coarsens bundles\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"The paper starts with a high similarity threshold, decreases delta and reclusters every three to five iterations, producing fewer and larger clusters once earlier iterations have already made edges locally elongated and partially bundled.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"CUDA image-space distance transforms, skeletonization, path computation, and attraction make the skeleton-based edge bundling pipeline substantially faster than a CPU AFMM implementation on the tested graphs.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"CUDA accelerates skeleton bundling\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"The CUDA implementation takes about 4 milliseconds for distance, feature transform, and skeletonization on the same image, about 0.8 seconds per iteration for the graph in Figure 7a-b, and is reported as roughly 100 times faster than the CPU-based AFMM.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Smoothing, relaxation, and cushion shading let users adjust whether bundles appear as sharp organic branches or smoother force-directed-like curves while preserving the computed connectivity pattern.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Smoothing controls bundle structure\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"Smoothing removes the skeleton effect from bundles, relaxation makes bundles thicker with less effect on curvature, and cushion shading modulates saturation and brightness using distance to the skeleton to help users follow overlapping bundles.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Skeleton-based edge bundling is most appropriate for coarse-scale connectivity exploration because bundling overdraws individual edges and distorts edge directions.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Bundling trades detail for structure\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"The limitations section states that any bundling inherently destroys information because edges are overdrawn, cannot be identified separately, and edge directions are distorted, so it should be used for coarse-scale connectivity patterns.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_items\": [\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-skeleton-bundling-pipeline--a18f4c9d72e6\",\n      \"title\": \"Skeleton Bundling Pipeline\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"The method proceeds by clustering edges into geometrically similar groups, computing a thin distance-field shape around each cluster, extracting the shape skeleton and feature transform, attracting the cluster edges toward the skeleton, iterating the process, and finally smoothing and rendering the result with cushion-like shading.\",\n      \"overview\": \"This formal pipeline is the core evidence that SBEB treats skeletons as layout cues rather than merely as visual shading aids. It establishes how clustering, distance transforms, skeletonization, attraction, iteration, and rendering fit together, while leaving final visual quality dependent on graph layout, parameters, and subjective readability.\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"formal_argument\",\n      \"claim_refs\": [\n        \"0\",\n        \"4\",\n        \"6\"\n      ],\n      \"source_id\": \"source-03-skeleton-based-edge-bundling-for-graph-visualization--1a3dfa0e6b4c\",\n      \"page_refs\": [\n        \"Fig. 1\",\n        \"Sec. 2\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-edge-cluster-distance--bb37a9e42d10\",\n      \"title\": \"Sampled Edge Distance\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"The clustering section defines d(e_i,e_j)=sqrt(sum_{k=1..N} ||e_ik-e_jk||''^2) over uniformly spaced edge samples, with N in [50,100], and reports that hierarchical bottom-up agglomerative clustering with full linkage and this Euclidean distance gives the best results.\",\n      \"overview\": \"This equation-level evidence explains how the method obtains elongated, coherent edge groups before skeletonization. Because the metric compares full sampled trajectories rather than only endpoints, it supports bundling curved or spatially similar edges, but it also means cluster quality depends on the initial layout and sampling resolution.\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"equation\",\n      \"claim_refs\": [\n        \"1\",\n        \"4\"\n      ],\n      \"source_id\": \"source-03-skeleton-based-edge-bundling-for-graph-visualization--1a3dfa0e6b4c\",\n      \"page_refs\": [\n        \"Eq. 1\",\n        \"Sec. 2.1\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-shape-skeleton-definitions--c4f08d2b61a9\",\n      \"title\": \"Distance-Field Skeleton Definition\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"The shape around a cluster is defined as Omega={x in R''^2 | DT_Delta(C)(x) <= omega}; its skeleton S_Omega is the set of points in Omega admitting at least two distinct boundary feature points at distance DT_boundary(Omega)(x), and the skeleton feature transform FT_S maps a point to a nearest skeleton feature point.\",\n      \"overview\": \"These definitions are the mathematical basis for using a skeleton as the centerline of a cluster'\"'s inflated edge shape. They show why the bundling target is locally centered, although the construction also depends on the chosen inflation radius omega and on image-space discretization.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"equation\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"0\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-skeleton-based-edge-bundling-for-graph-visualization--1a3dfa0e6b4c\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"Eq. 2\\\",\n        \\\"Eq. 3\\\",\n        \\\"Eq. 4\\\",\n        \\\"Eq. 5\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-attraction-endpoint-preservation--d9256b38aaf4\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Endpoint-Preserving Attraction Rule\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"The attraction rule is x_new = [1 - alpha phi(lambda(x)/lambda(e_ei))]x + alpha phi(lambda(x)/lambda(e_ei))FT_S(x), where phi(t)=[2 min(t,1-t)]\"'^K. The text states that edge endpoints do not move, nearby points move less, and points near the middle move most.\",\n      \"overview\": \"This evidence directly supports the claim that SBEB bends edges into bundles without moving graph nodes. The rule provides controllable tightness through alpha and curvature behavior through K, but repeated application can still distort edge direction as a cost of bundling.\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"equation\",\n      \"claim_refs\": [\n        \"2\",\n        \"7\"\n      ],\n      \"source_id\": \"source-03-skeleton-based-edge-bundling-for-graph-visualization--1a3dfa0e6b4c\",\n      \"page_refs\": [\n        \"Eq. 7\",\n        \"Eq. 8\",\n        \"Sec. 2.4\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-feature-transform-regularization--f71a0c6e2b55\",\n      \"title\": \"Path-Based Singularity Regularization\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"For each edge, the algorithm selects a skeleton path passing through the feature points of both edge endpoints. If a point'\"'s FT_S(x) is not on that path, the point is treated as special and mapped to a corresponding point on the path by arc-length interpolation before the attraction formula is applied.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This procedural evidence explains how the method avoids discontinuities that would otherwise create sharp kinks when edges cross Voronoi-like feature-transform boundaries. It supports the robustness claim for smooth bundles, while depending on correctly detected skeleton tips and paths.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"3\\\",\n        \\\"6\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-skeleton-based-edge-bundling-for-graph-visualization--1a3dfa0e6b4c\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"Sec. 2.4.1\\\",\n        \\\"Fig. 3\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-iterative-parameter-schedule--0e613cab8f27\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Progressive Reclustering Schedule\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"The iterative algorithm begins with a high similarity threshold to obtain thin coherent clusters, then decreases delta and reclusters every three to five iterations so that already bundled edges form fewer, larger, locally elongated clusters. The authors also state that alpha decreases from 0.9 to 0.2 and that I=15 is used in practice.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This evidence establishes why SBEB is progressive: it first creates local bundles and then lets those bundles merge into coarser structures. The parameter schedule gives practical defaults, but it also shows that results are controlled by heuristic choices rather than by a single optimality theorem.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"model_constraint\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"4\\\",\n        \\\"6\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-skeleton-based-edge-bundling-for-graph-visualization--1a3dfa0e6b4c\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"Sec. 2.5\\\",\n        \\\"Sec. 3\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-cuda-performance-tables--e89d4a6c31b2\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"CUDA Performance Measurements\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"Table 1 reports total GPU times of 6.3 seconds for US airlines, 4.1 seconds for US migrations, 7.4 seconds for Radial, 29.2 seconds for France air, and 5.2 seconds for Poker. Table 2 reports CUDA timings of 2 ms for inflation, 8 ms for holes, 2 ms for skeletonization, less than 12 ms for paths, and 3 ms for attraction.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The table values ground the performance claim in reported measurements rather than only complexity arguments. They show seconds-scale complete runs on several graph datasets and millisecond-scale GPU primitives, though the results are tied to the authors' hardware, image size, and implementation choices.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"table_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"5\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-skeleton-based-edge-bundling-for-graph-visualization--1a3dfa0e6b4c\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"Table 1\\\",\n        \\\"Table 2\\\",\n        \\\"Sec. 3.1\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-limitations-information-loss--9ac3b7d85021\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Bundling Information Loss\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"The discussion states that any bundling inherently destroys information: edges are overdrawn, individual edges cannot be identified separately, and edge directions are distorted. The recommended use is for applications interested in coarse-scale connectivity patterns where explicit graph simplification cannot be applied.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This limitation evidence is important because it bounds the interpretation of the method's visual results. SBEB improves structural readability for large graphs, but the paper explicitly cautions that bundled drawings should not be treated as preserving all individual-edge information.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"7\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-skeleton-based-edge-bundling-for-graph-visualization--1a3dfa0e6b4c\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"Sec. 5\\\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"relations\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"1\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"0\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Coherent geometric clustering is required before cluster skeletons can serve as meaningful bundling targets.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"derived_from\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"2\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"0\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The attraction rule operationalizes the central idea of moving clustered edges toward skeleton centerlines.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"3\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"6\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Regularized path attraction helps make the resulting bundles smooth enough for postprocessed visual exploration.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"4\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"0\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Iterative coarsening makes skeleton-guided bundling work at multiple structural scales.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"7\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"6\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Because bundling loses individual-edge detail, controls for smoothing and relaxation are important for practical interpretation.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Skeleton-based edge bundling constructs bundled layouts for general graphs by clustering geometrically similar edges, inflating each cluster into a distance-field shape, extracting that shape's skeleton, and attracting edges toward skeleton paths. The method is implemented with image-space GPU operations and is presented as a fast, controllable way to reveal coarse connectivity structure while accepting the information loss inherent in edge bundling.\\\",\n  \\\"warnings\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"code\\\": \\\"missing_publication_date\\\",\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"Publication date could not be resolved from the source record; source_date_inference.date is null.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"source_dossier\\\": {\n    \\\"summary_short\\\": \\\"This source introduces skeleton-based edge bundling, which uses medial axes of inflated edge clusters as graph-layout guides. It combines sampled-edge clustering, distance-field skeletons, endpoint-preserving attraction, GPU acceleration, and visual postprocessing to reveal coarse connectivity while obscuring individual edge detail.\\\",\n    \\\"summary_long\\\": \\\"Ersoy, Hurter, Paulovich, Cantareira, and Telea propose skeleton-based edge bundling for visualizing large general graphs whose node positions are already known. The central idea is to turn groups of geometrically similar edges into thin image-space shapes and then use each shape's medial-axis skeleton as the centerline toward which the edges should be attracted. This reframes bundling as a sequence of edge clustering, distance-transform construction, skeleton extraction, skeleton-path selection, and endpoint-preserving edge advection. The paper's reasoning path is mathematical and procedural: sampled-edge distances create coherent clusters; distance fields inflate cluster drawings into shapes; skeletons identify local centers of those shapes; feature transforms provide attraction directions; and a regularization step prevents singular attraction artifacts from becoming visible kinks. The implementation emphasizes graphics hardware, using CUDA distance and feature transforms, hole filling, skeletonization, path computation, and independent edge attraction to achieve seconds-scale performance on several graph examples. The result is a method that can produce organic, branching bundles or smoother force-directed-like curves depending on smoothing and relaxation choices. Its practical interpretation is deliberately limited: the visual output helps users see coarse routes, topic-to-topic citation structure, air traffic corridors, or migration-like patterns, but it does not preserve individual edge identity or exact direction. The paper is therefore strongest as a layout and interaction technique for structural overview, not as a lossless representation of graph topology.\\\",\n    \\\"sections\\\": [\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Core Argument\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The paper argues that the skeleton of an inflated edge-cluster shape is a natural bundling guide because skeletons are locally centered within elongated shapes. Once similar edges are clustered, their surrounding distance-field shape captures a shared corridor, and its skeleton provides the central path along which those edges can be drawn.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"0\\\",\n          \\\"1\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Clustering And Shape Construction\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The first technical step compares uniformly sampled edge trajectories and uses hierarchical agglomerative clustering to find spatially close, same-direction edge groups. Each cluster drawing is then inflated by a distance threshold into a compact shape, making skeletonization possible even though the original input consists of graph edges rather than filled regions.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"1\\\",\n          \\\"0\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Attraction Mechanics\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The attraction formula moves sampled edge points toward skeleton feature-transform locations, with an arc-length weighting that leaves endpoints fixed and moves middle points most strongly. This is essential for graph drawings because nodes must remain at their original positions while edges bend into shared bundles.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"2\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Robustness Measures\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"A direct feature-transform advection field can create sharp kinks near discontinuities. The paper addresses this by assigning each edge to a skeleton path through its endpoint feature points and remapping special off-path attraction cases onto that path, making the attraction smoother and more stable.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"3\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Progressive Bundling\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"SBEB is not a one-shot operation: it reclusters repeatedly as edges move closer together. A high initial similarity threshold creates reliable fine-scale bundles, then decreasing thresholds allow those already coherent bundles to merge into larger structures without immediately grouping unrelated crossing edges.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"4\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Implementation And Performance\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The implementation treats shapes, skeletons, distance transforms, and attraction largely as image-space operations. Reported table measurements show millisecond-scale CUDA primitives and total GPU times of a few to tens of seconds across tested datasets, supporting the claim that hardware acceleration is central to the method's practicality.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"5\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Visual Control\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The paper gives users postprocessing controls over smoothing, relaxation, and cushion shading. These controls matter because skeleton-guided layouts can look sharply organic or smoother and more force-directed-like, and shaded profiles help users follow overlapping bundles without changing the computed screen-space thickness.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"6\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Limits And Interpretation\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The authors explicitly caution that bundling destroys information by overdraw, loss of individual-edge identity, and direction distortion. The method should therefore be interpreted as a tool for overview and coarse connectivity analysis, especially when graph simplification is unavailable or unsuitable.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"7\\\"\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\nJSON\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
